---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { facts } from '../data/facts';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Knowledge platform — Andrew Yang"
  description="How a personal repository fleet is governed by one operator: convention plugins, automated dependency policy, and an idempotent provisioning pipeline."
>
  <h1>A {facts.repoCount}-repository platform with one operator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    My reading notes, handbooks, and topic sites live in {facts.repoCount}+ repositories under
    one GitHub organization. At that scale, "personal websites" becomes a fleet
    management problem — which is exactly why it's here.
  </p>

  <h2>The engineering underneath</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>Fleet governance:</strong> a Gradle convention plugin published to a
      package registry pins toolchain and formatting for every repository; a shared
      Renovate preset rolls dependency updates across the fleet monthly with automerge.
      The plugin's version is declared once per repository as a range rather than a
      pinned string, so publishing the plugin <em>is</em> the rollout — the reasoning,
      and the 36 GB of duplicated build artifacts that forced it, are in
      <a href={`${base}/lab/convention-by-reference/`}>a convention copied 1,600 times</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Provisioning pipeline:</strong> a Kotlin CLI takes a queue of books and
      creates repositories end-to-end — AI-assisted metadata, duplicate detection
      against a cached repo index, template instantiation, cover processing, docs
      scaffolding, and GitHub Pages activation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Metadata discipline:</strong> a three-tier topic taxonomy with a
      whitelist keeps {facts.repoCount}+ repositories queryable; idempotent provisioning makes
      re-runs safe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Operational scar tissue:</strong> batch pushes hitting Pages deploy
      throttling, formatting hooks across ~1,300 working copies, wrapper upgrades
      across generations of JDKs, a fleet-wide sweep that never converged because
      new repositories were being provisioned while it ran — fleet problems, fleet
      solutions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>v1 → v2 → v3 of the same idea</h2>
  <p>
    The provisioning CLI is a file-based queue with a two-phase AI workflow — an
    async content pipeline, version one.
    <a href={`${base}/demo/bookshelf-echo/`}>bookshelf-echo</a> is version
    two of the same problem with production-grade semantics: a real broker,
    delivery guarantees, retry topology, and observability. Version three adds
    the thing versions one and two were missing — editorial judgment backed by
    my reading corpus. The full arc:
    <a href={`${base}/lab/async-pipeline-three-generations/`}>three generations
    of the same pipeline</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
